// Step 13 - OpenAI Realtime API: Play Back the Reply
// Extends step 12: output_modalities switches back to ["audio"] (it was
// ["text"] in steps 11-12 to sidestep the heap risk - see below), and each
// LISTEN cycle now decodes and plays the model's spoken reply through the
// MAX98357A speaker, one small chunk at a time, rather than ever buffering
// the whole reply in RAM.
//
// Requires the "WebSockets" library by Markus Sattler (Links2004) and
// "ArduinoJson" by Benoit Blanchon (v7), both installable via the Arduino
// Library Manager. Base64 encoding/decoding uses mbedTLS, bundled with the
// ESP32 Arduino core - no extra library needed for that.
//
// ----- Shared I2S port, not two independent ones -----
// This board's BCLK/LRC clock lines are physically shared between the mic
// and the DAC (I2S_BCLK/I2S_LRC in config.h are the *same* GPIOs as
// I2S_MIC_SERIAL_CLOCK/I2S_MIC_LEFT_RIGHT_CLOCK - 26/25). Old step 4
// discovered that two independent I2S masters fight over those shared
// pins, and fixed it by running ONE port in full-duplex (RX+TX) mode
// instead. Step 12 got away with a separate RX-only I2S_NUM_1 because
// nothing was using I2S_NUM_0/TX yet - now that playback needs TX too,
// this switches to I2S_NUM_0 in full duplex, following step 4's proven
// pattern rather than two ports.
//
// Full duplex on this driver means RX and TX share one sample rate, so
// both directions run the peripheral at 24000Hz - the input floor step 10
// discovered. Output audio itself now arrives at 8kHz (see "8kHz mu-law"
// below) and gets upsampled to match before it ever reaches the ring
// buffer/I2S, so the peripheral's single shared rate is still satisfied.
//
// ----- Not blocking inside the WebSocket callback -----
// i2s_write() can block waiting for DMA buffer space - old step 8c's
// design note (carried in every WS sketch since step 9) warns this is the
// same category of risk as the sendTXT()-from-callback deadlock found in
// step 7. So handleServerMessage() (called from the WS library's own
// callback) never calls i2s_write() itself - it only decodes each
// response.output_audio.delta chunk straight into a small ring buffer.
// loop() (safely outside the WS library's own call stack) drains that
// ring buffer to the speaker a chunk at a time.
//
// ----- Audio blew the heap, again - turned out not to be heap at all -----
// Step 11 guessed that a full deserializeJson() on a large
// response.output_audio.delta payload exhausted heap and killed the
// connection. First real attempt at this step (24kHz PCM output, same as
// the session config step 10 validated) reused that guess: findRawStringField()
// locates the "delta" field's base64 span directly inside the WebSocket
// library's own already-allocated payload buffer (no copy, no
// JsonDocument), and decodeAndRingAudioDelta() base64-decodes it in small
// fixed-size aligned slices straight into the ring buffer - the same
// "never allocate proportionally to the delta's size" discipline as
// before. It still disconnected, every time, before a single audio delta
// ever arrived - and this time with hard diagnostics proving it wasn't
// memory: free heap stayed ~150-155KB and ESP.getMaxAllocHeap() (largest
// *contiguous* free block, the real number that matters for one big
// allocation, not just total free bytes) stayed 59-63KB, both comfortably
// above the ~26KB a single 24kHz-PCM audio delta frame needed. A
// standalone Python script (tools/realtime_audio_diagnostic.py)
// reproducing the identical session.update/conversation.item.create/
// response.create flow against the same API succeeded cleanly every time,
// ruling out the server too. That script's own log also revealed OpenAI
// chunks output audio into fixed ~0.4s pieces (25600 base64 chars per
// chunk at 24kHz PCM) sent as single, non-fragmented WS frames - and a
// near-identical bug report in a *different* ESP32 Arduino WebSocket
// library (github.com/gilmaimon/ArduinoWebsockets/issues/21) has its
// maintainer concluding, after investigating, "this looks like a bug in
// the library itself, it has nothing to do with memory restrictions."
// Best explanation left: this WebSockets (Links2004) library has some
// protocol-handling limit with single frames around that size, on this
// board - not a documented one, not something we can fix inside the
// library from here, but real all the same.
//
// ----- 8kHz mu-law: shrinking the frame instead of fighting the library -----
// OpenAI's chunking is a fixed *duration* (~0.4s), not a fixed byte count,
// so the fix is to lower the bitrate we ask for rather than touch the
// WebSocket layer at all: session.update now requests "audio/pcmu" (G.711
// mu-law, 8kHz, 1 byte/sample) for OUTPUT only - mic input stays 24kHz PCM
// exactly as before. Confirmed on real hardware: chunks dropped from
// 25600 base64 chars to 4268, and the disconnect is gone entirely -
// deltas now arrive every time. ulawToLinearPcm16() decodes each byte with
// the standard public-domain ITU-T G.711 bit-manipulation algorithm (not a
// lookup table); 8kHz mu-law is telephone-quality audio, a real drop from
// 24kHz, but plenty intelligible for a voice assistant.
//
// ----- Ring buffer stores compressed audio, not PCM -----
// First attempt at playback decoded+upsampled each delta to 24kHz PCM
// *before* ringing it (so the ring buffer would stay a simple, generic
// byte queue) - and immediately hit a second bug: the ring filled and
// stayed full within 3 deltas (~90ms), with a wall of "Ring buffer
// overflow" logs after that. Root cause: OpenAI sends the whole reply's
// audio over the network far faster than real-time playback can consume
// it (that's normal for a data transfer, not paced to a DAC), and decoded
// 24kHz PCM at 32KB only holds ~0.68s - nowhere near enough headroom to
// absorb that burst for any reply longer than well under a second. Fixed
// by ringing the raw mu-law bytes instead (decodeAndRingAudioDelta() is
// back to a plain base64-decode-and-ringWrite, same as before mu-law was
// added) and moving the decode+3x upsample (kOutputUpsampleFactor, 8kHz ->
// 24kHz, simple nearest-neighbor repetition) into drainRingBufferToSpeaker()
// instead, right before i2s_write(). Mu-law's compactness is the whole
// win here: the same 32KB now holds ~4.1s of audio instead of ~0.68s -
// generous headroom for a real reply, for free, just by buffering the
// wire format instead of the decoded one.
//
// Wi-Fi and OpenAI-WSS plumbing (connect, reconnect backoff, generic event
// logging) live in wifi_utils.h/.cpp and openai_realtime_utils.h/.cpp,
// shared with every other test sketch in this folder.

#include <WebSocketsClient.h>
#include <ArduinoJson.h>
#include <driver/i2s.h>
#include <mbedtls/base64.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "config.h"
#include "wifi_utils.h"
#include "openai_realtime_utils.h"

namespace {
// output_modalities:["audio"] again (see header comment). Input stays PCM
// at 24000Hz - the floor step 10 discovered, and what the mic already
// captures. Output switches to 8kHz mu-law ("audio/pcmu", no rate field -
// G.711 mu-law is fixed at 8kHz) instead of 24kHz PCM - see the "8kHz
// mu-law" header note for why. turn_detection stays off (manual control,
// per step 12).
const char kSessionUpdateMessage[] =
    R"JSON({"type":"session.update","session":{"type":"realtime","model":")JSON"
    OPENAI_MODEL
    R"JSON(","instructions":"You are a helpful, concise voice assistant running on an ESP32 dev board. Keep spoken replies short.","output_modalities":["audio"],"audio":{"input":{"format":{"type":"audio/pcm","rate":24000},"turn_detection":null},"output":{"format":{"type":"audio/pcmu"},"voice":"alloy"}},"reasoning":{"effort":"low"}}})JSON";

const char kCommitMessage[] = R"JSON({"type":"input_audio_buffer.commit"})JSON";
const char kResponseCreateMessage[] = R"JSON({"type":"response.create"})JSON";

// ----- I2S (shared full-duplex port - see header comment) -----
constexpr i2s_port_t kI2sPort = I2S_NUM_0;
constexpr uint32_t kSampleRateHz = 24000;
constexpr uint32_t kRecordDurationSec = 3;  // same duration old step 8b/step 12 used
constexpr uint32_t kChunkSamples = 2400;    // ~100ms per chunk at 24kHz - same chunk duration step 12 confirmed working
constexpr uint32_t kI2sDmaBufLen = 300;
constexpr uint32_t kI2sDmaBufCount = 12;  // 12*300 = 3600 samples of ring capacity, comfortably above kChunkSamples
constexpr int32_t kSoftwareGain = 4;      // mic levels are otherwise quiet - see step 3 findings
constexpr unsigned long kReplyTimeoutMs = 15000;

constexpr uint32_t kChunkPcmBytes = kChunkSamples * sizeof(int16_t);
constexpr uint32_t kBase64BufferSize = ((kChunkPcmBytes + 2) / 3) * 4 + 1;
constexpr uint32_t kJsonMessageBufferSize = kBase64BufferSize + 128;

// ----- Playback ring buffer -----
// Holds raw mu-law bytes (NOT decoded PCM) - see "Ring buffer stores
// compressed audio, not PCM" below for why. At 8kHz/1 byte-per-sample,
// 32KB is ~4.1s of audio - bounded headroom for a real reply, not the
// literal whole thing, but generous since the format is so compact.
constexpr uint32_t kRingBufferBytes = 32 * 1024;
// Per-pass base64 decode size, aligned to base64's 4-character grouping -
// caps how much of one delta gets decoded at a time, regardless of how
// large the delta itself is.
constexpr uint32_t kDecodeInputChars = 1024;
constexpr uint32_t kDecodeOutputBufBytes = 800;  // >= 1024/4*3 = 768 decoded mu-law bytes
// mu-law is 8kHz; the shared full-duplex I2S peripheral runs at 24kHz (see
// header comment) - repeat each decoded sample this many times.
constexpr int kOutputUpsampleFactor = 3;
// Drained from the ring to the speaker per loop() pass, in raw mu-law
// bytes - 320 bytes = 40ms of source audio (960 samples once upsampled),
// a deliberately small per-call chunk so i2s_write()'s blocking wait for
// DMA space (see header comment on not blocking too long) stays short.
constexpr uint32_t kTxDrainMuLawBytes = 320;

// Static, not stack-local: these buffers would overflow the default
// Arduino loop task stack if declared inside a function (same reasoning
// as old step 8b/step 12).
int32_t raw_chunk[kChunkSamples];
int16_t pcm_chunk[kChunkSamples];
unsigned char base64_chunk[kBase64BufferSize];
char json_message[kJsonMessageBufferSize];
uint8_t ring_buffer[kRingBufferBytes];
uint8_t decode_scratch[kDecodeOutputBufBytes];
uint8_t tx_mulaw_bytes[kTxDrainMuLawBytes];
int32_t tx_i2s_words[kTxDrainMuLawBytes * kOutputUpsampleFactor];

WebSocketsClient webSocket;
bool should_keep_running = true;   // only false if the connection itself can never be established
bool should_send_session_update = false;
bool session_ready = false;        // true once session.updated has been received at least once
bool session_ever_ready = false;
bool is_capturing = false;
bool awaiting_reply = false;
bool reply_received_this_cycle = false;
bool transcript_in_progress = false;  // a response.output_audio_transcript.delta stream is currently mid-line
bool response_fully_received = false; // response.done arrived - ring buffer may still be draining
uint32_t listen_cycle = 0;
unsigned long reply_deadline_ms = 0;
unsigned long reply_wait_started_ms = 0;  // diagnostic: elapsed-time-since-response-requested at disconnect

uint32_t ring_write_index = 0;
uint32_t ring_read_index = 0;
uint32_t ring_used_bytes = 0;
}  // namespace

/** Drives the onboard LED, respecting this board's active-high/low polarity. */
void setLed(bool on) {
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, (on == LED_ACTIVE_HIGH) ? HIGH : LOW);
}

/** Marks this LISTEN cycle as having gotten a reply, logging the verdict exactly once per cycle. */
void markReplyReceived() {
  if (reply_received_this_cycle) return;
  reply_received_this_cycle = true;
  awaiting_reply = false;
  Serial.printf("[openai_playback] ===== LISTEN #%u PASSED =====\n", listen_cycle);
}

/** Marks this LISTEN cycle as failed, logging the verdict exactly once per cycle.
    Reserved for a genuine problem (protocol error, failed response, timeout) - not
    logged at all if a reply already arrived for this cycle. */
void markReplyFailed(const char* reason) {
  if (reply_received_this_cycle || !awaiting_reply) return;
  reply_received_this_cycle = true;
  awaiting_reply = false;
  Serial.printf("[openai_playback] ===== LISTEN #%u FAILED: %s =====\n", listen_cycle, reason);
}

/** Halts the sketch entirely - reserved for the connection itself never succeeding
    (see openai_realtime_utils's attempt cap), not for a single LISTEN cycle failing. */
void haltSketch(const char* reason) {
  should_keep_running = false;
  Serial.printf("[openai_playback] ===== HALTED: %s =====\n", reason);
}

/** Resets the playback ring buffer to empty - called at the start of each LISTEN
    cycle and on disconnect, so stale/partial audio from a previous cycle never
    gets mixed into the next one. */
void resetRingBuffer() {
  ring_write_index = 0;
  ring_read_index = 0;
  ring_used_bytes = 0;
}

/** Bytes free in the ring buffer right now. */
uint32_t ringFreeBytes() {
  return kRingBufferBytes - ring_used_bytes;
}

/** Copies len bytes into the ring buffer, wrapping as needed. Drops (and logs) the
    whole segment rather than partially writing or blocking if it doesn't fit -
    a full ring means playback can't keep up with the network, which is worth
    knowing about, not silently smoothing over. */
void ringWrite(const uint8_t* data, uint32_t len) {
  if (len > ringFreeBytes()) {
    Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Ring buffer overflow - dropping %u bytes of decoded audio.\n", (unsigned)len);
    return;
  }
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < len; i++) {
    ring_buffer[ring_write_index] = data[i];
    ring_write_index = (ring_write_index + 1) % kRingBufferBytes;
  }
  ring_used_bytes += len;
}

/** Copies up to max_len bytes out of the ring buffer into out, wrapping as needed.
    Returns how many bytes were actually available and copied. */
uint32_t ringRead(uint8_t* out, uint32_t max_len) {
  uint32_t n = min(max_len, ring_used_bytes);
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    out[i] = ring_buffer[ring_read_index];
    ring_read_index = (ring_read_index + 1) % kRingBufferBytes;
  }
  ring_used_bytes -= n;
  return n;
}

/** Configures I2S0 as a full-duplex master: RX from the INMP441, TX to the
    MAX98357A - one port, not two, since BCLK/LRC are physically shared
    between them (see header comment). */
void setupI2sFullDuplex() {
  i2s_config_t i2s_config = {
    .mode = static_cast<i2s_mode_t>(I2S_MODE_MASTER | I2S_MODE_TX | I2S_MODE_RX),
    .sample_rate = kSampleRateHz,
    .bits_per_sample = I2S_BITS_PER_SAMPLE_32BIT,  // INMP441 sends 24-bit data in a 32-bit frame; TX bus matches width
    .channel_format = I2S_CHANNEL_FMT_ONLY_RIGHT,  // confirmed in step 3 for this board's mic
    .communication_format = I2S_COMM_FORMAT_STAND_I2S,
    .intr_alloc_flags = ESP_INTR_FLAG_LEVEL1,
    .dma_buf_count = kI2sDmaBufCount,
    .dma_buf_len = kI2sDmaBufLen,
    .use_apll = false,
    .tx_desc_auto_clear = true,  // auto-fills silence between writes, avoiding TX buffer underrun noise
    .fixed_mclk = 0
  };

  i2s_pin_config_t pin_config = {
    .bck_io_num = I2S_BCLK,             // shared clock line, also wired to the mic
    .ws_io_num = I2S_LRC,               // shared clock line, also wired to the mic
    .data_out_num = I2S_DOUT,           // to MAX98357A DIN
    .data_in_num = I2S_MIC_SERIAL_DATA  // from INMP441 SD
  };

  esp_err_t install_result = i2s_driver_install(kI2sPort, &i2s_config, 0, nullptr);
  if (install_result != ESP_OK) {
    // Continuing to i2s_set_pin() after a failed install previously caused a
    // Guru Meditation crash-reboot loop for this exact driver (see old step
    // 8b's dma_buf_len bug in PROJECT_OVERVIEW.md) - fail loudly instead.
    Serial.printf("[openai_playback] i2s_driver_install failed (code %d) - halting.\n", (int)install_result);
    while (true) delay(1000);
  }
  i2s_set_pin(kI2sPort, &pin_config);
}

/** Cheaply extracts the "type" field's value from a raw JSON payload without a
    full JSON parse, by scanning for the literal `"type":"..."` that every
    event seen so far starts with. Returns false if not found within the
    first 128 bytes (which "type" always is, for every event observed). */
bool extractEventType(const uint8_t* payload, size_t length, char* out, size_t out_size) {
  constexpr char kNeedle[] = "\"type\":\"";
  constexpr size_t kNeedleLen = sizeof(kNeedle) - 1;
  constexpr size_t kScanWindow = 128;
  size_t scan_limit = length < kScanWindow ? length : kScanWindow;

  for (size_t i = 0; i + kNeedleLen <= scan_limit; i++) {
    if (memcmp(payload + i, kNeedle, kNeedleLen) != 0) continue;
    size_t start = i + kNeedleLen;
    size_t j = start;
    while (j < length && payload[j] != '"' && (j - start) < out_size - 1) j++;
    memcpy(out, payload + start, j - start);
    out[j - start] = '\0';
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

/** Finds a `"field_name":"..."` string field anywhere in a raw JSON payload and
    returns a pointer/length into the ORIGINAL buffer - no copy - so a large
    value (like a base64 audio blob) is never duplicated in RAM just to read
    it. Safe for base64 content specifically: that alphabet never contains a
    literal `"`, so the first following quote is always the true end. */
bool findRawStringField(const uint8_t* payload, size_t length, const char* field_name,
                         const uint8_t** value_start, size_t* value_len) {
  char needle[32];
  int needle_len = snprintf(needle, sizeof(needle), "\"%s\":\"", field_name);
  if (needle_len <= 0 || (size_t)needle_len >= sizeof(needle)) return false;

  for (size_t i = 0; i + (size_t)needle_len <= length; i++) {
    if (memcmp(payload + i, needle, needle_len) != 0) continue;
    size_t start = i + needle_len;
    size_t j = start;
    while (j < length && payload[j] != '"') j++;
    if (j >= length) return false;
    *value_start = payload + start;
    *value_len = j - start;
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

/** ITU-T G.711 mu-law sample -> linear PCM16. The classic public-domain
    bit-manipulation decode (not a lookup table) - see the header comment's
    "8kHz mu-law" note. */
int16_t ulawToLinearPcm16(uint8_t ulaw_sample) {
  constexpr int kBias = 0x84;
  uint8_t inverted = ~ulaw_sample;
  int magnitude = ((inverted & 0x0F) << 3) + kBias;
  magnitude <<= (inverted & 0x70) >> 4;
  return static_cast<int16_t>((inverted & 0x80) ? (kBias - magnitude) : (magnitude - kBias));
}

/** Base64-decodes a (possibly large) span of mu-law audio in small fixed-size
    aligned slices, writing the raw mu-law bytes straight into the ring
    buffer - decode-to-PCM and upsampling happen later, at drain time (see
    "Ring buffer stores compressed audio, not PCM" in the header comment).
    See the "Audio blew the heap, again" note for why this never allocates
    proportionally to the delta's own size. */
void decodeAndRingAudioDelta(const uint8_t* b64_data, size_t b64_len) {
  size_t offset = 0;
  while (offset < b64_len) {
    size_t take = min((size_t)kDecodeInputChars, b64_len - offset);
    take -= take % 4;  // stay aligned to base64's 4-character groups
    if (take == 0) break;

    size_t decoded_len = 0;
    int result = mbedtls_base64_decode(decode_scratch, sizeof(decode_scratch), &decoded_len,
                                        b64_data + offset, take);
    if (result != 0) {
      Serial.printf("[openai_playback] base64 decode failed (code %d) - dropping remainder of this delta.\n", result);
      return;
    }
    ringWrite(decode_scratch, decoded_len);
    offset += take;
  }
}

/** Parses one server text frame, logs its "type" and any interesting fields, and
    drives session readiness plus the current LISTEN cycle's PASS/FAIL verdict. */
void handleServerMessage(uint8_t* payload, size_t length) {
  char type_buf[48];
  bool have_type = extractEventType(payload, length, type_buf, sizeof(type_buf));

  if (have_type && strcmp(type_buf, "response.output_audio.delta") == 0) {
    const uint8_t* value_start = nullptr;
    size_t value_len = 0;
    if (findRawStringField(payload, length, "delta", &value_start, &value_len)) {
      // Diagnostic (not yet confirmed necessary): logs the raw frame size and
      // free heap for every audio delta, so if a disconnect follows one of
      // these we can tell whether it was the frame itself (WebSocketsClient
      // must buffer the whole thing before this callback ever runs, so a
      // large single frame could exhaust heap regardless of how cheaply we
      // then parse it) versus cumulative pressure from many smaller ones.
      Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Event type: response.output_audio.delta (%u bytes, base64 span %u). Free heap: %u, largest free block: %u\n",
                    (unsigned)length, (unsigned)value_len, ESP.getFreeHeap(), ESP.getMaxAllocHeap());
      decodeAndRingAudioDelta(value_start, value_len);
    } else {
      Serial.println("[openai_playback] Event type: response.output_audio.delta, but no delta field found.");
    }
    return;
  }

  if (have_type && strncmp(type_buf, "response.output_audio", 22) == 0) {
    // Any other response.output_audio* event (e.g. .done) carries no large
    // payload, but there's nothing this step needs from it either - just
    // log its size for visibility.
    Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Event type: %s (%u bytes).\n", type_buf, (unsigned)length);
    return;
  }

  JsonDocument doc;
  DeserializationError err = deserializeJson(doc, reinterpret_cast<const char*>(payload), length);
  if (err) {
    Serial.printf("[openai_playback] JSON parse error: %s\n", err.c_str());
    markReplyFailed("received a message that isn't valid JSON");
    return;
  }

  const char* type = doc["type"] | "(missing type)";

  if (strcmp(type, "session.created") == 0) {
    Serial.println("[openai_playback] Event type: session.created (server-initiated, before our own update).");
    return;
  }

  if (strcmp(type, "session.updated") == 0) {
    session_ready = true;
    Serial.println("[openai_playback] Event type: session.updated -> ready. Type LISTEN to record and send audio.");
    if (!session_ever_ready) {
      session_ever_ready = true;
      // A later mid-conversation disconnect gets its own fresh attempt
      // budget, rather than one shared with however many retries startup
      // itself needed.
      resetReconnectBackoff();
    }
    return;
  }

  if (strcmp(type, "response.output_audio_transcript.delta") == 0) {
    const char* delta = doc["delta"] | "";
    if (!transcript_in_progress) {
      transcript_in_progress = true;
      Serial.print("[openai_playback] Event type: response.output_audio_transcript.delta -> streaming transcript: ");
    }
    Serial.print(delta);
    return;
  }

  if (strcmp(type, "response.output_audio_transcript.done") == 0) {
    if (transcript_in_progress) {
      Serial.println();
      transcript_in_progress = false;
    }
    const char* transcript = doc["transcript"] | (const char*)nullptr;
    Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Event type: response.output_audio_transcript.done -> full transcript: \"%s\"\n",
                  transcript != nullptr ? transcript : "(none)");
    return;
  }

  if (strcmp(type, "response.done") == 0) {
    const char* status = doc["response"]["status"] | "(unknown)";
    Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Event type: response.done -> status: %s\n", status);
    if (strcmp(status, "failed") == 0) {
      const char* error_message = doc["response"]["status_details"]["error"]["message"] | "(no error detail)";
      markReplyFailed(error_message);
    } else {
      // The PASS verdict itself waits for the ring buffer to actually finish
      // draining to the speaker - see checkPlaybackComplete() in loop().
      response_fully_received = true;
    }
    return;
  }

  if (strcmp(type, "error") == 0) {
    const char* message = doc["error"]["message"] | "(no message)";
    Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Event type: error -> %s\n", message);
    markReplyFailed(message);
    return;
  }

  Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Event type: %s (unhandled/unexpected for this step, ignoring).\n", type);
}

/** Handles WebSocket lifecycle events. CONNECTED/DISCONNECTED/TEXT get
    explicit handling here; everything else is delegated to
    logGenericWsEvent() so nothing goes unnoticed. */
void onWebSocketEvent(WStype_t type, uint8_t* payload, size_t length) {
  switch (type) {
    case WStype_CONNECTED:
      Serial.println("[openai_playback] WSS handshake succeeded - connection is open.");
      session_ready = false;
      // Deferred to loop() rather than sent here: calling sendTXT() directly
      // inside this callback can deadlock the library (discovered in old
      // step 7 - see PROJECT_OVERVIEW.md "Discoveries").
      should_send_session_update = true;
      break;
    case WStype_DISCONNECTED:
      Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Free heap at disconnect: %u, largest free block: %u\n",
                    ESP.getFreeHeap(), ESP.getMaxAllocHeap());
      if (awaiting_reply) {
        Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Elapsed since response.create was sent: %lu ms\n",
                      millis() - reply_wait_started_ms);
      }
      session_ready = false;
      resetRingBuffer();  // any partial reply audio is now stale - don't play it
      markReplyFailed("disconnected before a reply arrived");
      if (registerDisconnectAndMaybeGiveUp(webSocket)) {
        haltSketch("connection never succeeded after repeated attempts");
      }
      break;
    case WStype_TEXT:
      handleServerMessage(payload, length);
      break;
    default:
      logGenericWsEvent(type, payload, length);
      break;
  }
}

/** Records kRecordDurationSec of mic audio, streaming each ~100ms chunk to the
    session as it's captured. LED on throughout. Commits the buffer and
    requests a response once done, then starts the reply timeout. */
void captureAndStreamAudio() {
  listen_cycle++;
  is_capturing = true;
  awaiting_reply = false;
  reply_received_this_cycle = false;
  response_fully_received = false;
  resetRingBuffer();
  setLed(true);
  Serial.printf("[openai_playback] LISTEN #%u: recording %us and streaming to OpenAI...\n",
                listen_cycle, kRecordDurationSec);

  uint32_t total_samples = kSampleRateHz * kRecordDurationSec;
  uint32_t samples_sent = 0;
  uint32_t chunks_sent = 0;

  // Independent of whether the model replies, this tells us whether real
  // audio was actually captured - separates "mic/gain problem" from "server
  // never responded" as possible explanations for a FAILED verdict.
  int16_t level_min = INT16_MAX;
  int16_t level_max = INT16_MIN;
  double level_sum_of_squares = 0.0;

  while (samples_sent < total_samples) {
    uint32_t samples_wanted = min(kChunkSamples, total_samples - samples_sent);
    size_t bytes_read = 0;
    i2s_read(kI2sPort, raw_chunk, samples_wanted * sizeof(int32_t), &bytes_read, portMAX_DELAY);
    uint32_t samples_read = bytes_read / sizeof(int32_t);

    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < samples_read; i++) {
      int16_t sample16 = static_cast<int16_t>(raw_chunk[i] >> 16);
      int32_t scaled = static_cast<int32_t>(sample16) * kSoftwareGain;
      if (scaled > INT16_MAX) scaled = INT16_MAX;
      if (scaled < INT16_MIN) scaled = INT16_MIN;
      pcm_chunk[i] = static_cast<int16_t>(scaled);
      level_min = min(level_min, pcm_chunk[i]);
      level_max = max(level_max, pcm_chunk[i]);
      level_sum_of_squares += static_cast<double>(pcm_chunk[i]) * pcm_chunk[i];
    }

    size_t base64_len = 0;
    int b64_result = mbedtls_base64_encode(base64_chunk, sizeof(base64_chunk), &base64_len,
                                            reinterpret_cast<const unsigned char*>(pcm_chunk),
                                            samples_read * sizeof(int16_t));
    if (b64_result != 0) {
      Serial.printf("[openai_playback] base64 encode failed (code %d) - skipping this chunk.\n", b64_result);
      samples_sent += samples_read;
      continue;
    }

    snprintf(json_message, kJsonMessageBufferSize,
             R"JSON({"type":"input_audio_buffer.append","audio":"%.*s"})JSON",
             (int)base64_len, base64_chunk);
    webSocket.sendTXT(json_message);

    samples_sent += samples_read;
    chunks_sent++;
  }

  webSocket.sendTXT(kCommitMessage);
  webSocket.sendTXT(kResponseCreateMessage);
  setLed(false);
  double level_rms = sqrt(level_sum_of_squares / samples_sent);
  Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Done streaming (%u chunks). Mic level: min=%d max=%d rms=%.1f\n",
                chunks_sent, level_min, level_max, level_rms);
  Serial.println("[openai_playback] Waiting for the reply...");

  is_capturing = false;
  awaiting_reply = true;
  reply_wait_started_ms = millis();
  reply_deadline_ms = reply_wait_started_ms + kReplyTimeoutMs;
}

/** Checks Serial for a LISTEN command and starts a capture if the session is
    ready and idle (not still capturing OR still playing back a previous reply). */
void checkForListenCommand() {
  if (!Serial.available()) {
    return;
  }
  String command = Serial.readStringUntil('\n');
  command.trim();
  if (!command.equalsIgnoreCase("LISTEN")) {
    return;
  }

  if (!session_ready) {
    Serial.println("[openai_playback] Not ready yet - still connecting/setting up.");
  } else if (is_capturing || awaiting_reply) {
    Serial.println("[openai_playback] Still recording or playing back a reply - ignoring.");
  } else {
    captureAndStreamAudio();
  }
}

/** Checks whether the reply timeout for the current LISTEN cycle has expired. */
void checkReplyTimeout() {
  if (!awaiting_reply) {
    return;
  }
  if (millis() < reply_deadline_ms) {
    return;
  }
  char reason[48];
  snprintf(reason, sizeof(reason), "no reply within %lu ms", kReplyTimeoutMs);
  markReplyFailed(reason);
}

/** Drains up to kTxDrainMuLawBytes of raw mu-law audio from the ring buffer,
    decodes and upsamples it (see "Ring buffer stores compressed audio, not
    PCM" in the header comment), and writes it to the speaker. Safe to call
    every loop() pass - a no-op when the ring is empty, and only ever blocks
    as long as i2s_write() naturally does for one small (40ms) chunk (this
    runs outside the WebSocket library's own call stack, so blocking briefly
    here is fine - see header comment). */
void drainRingBufferToSpeaker() {
  uint32_t n = ringRead(tx_mulaw_bytes, kTxDrainMuLawBytes);
  if (n == 0) return;

  uint32_t word_index = 0;
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    int32_t widened = static_cast<int32_t>(ulawToLinearPcm16(tx_mulaw_bytes[i])) << 16;  // re-align for the 32-bit-wide TX bus
    for (int rep = 0; rep < kOutputUpsampleFactor; rep++) {
      tx_i2s_words[word_index++] = widened;
    }
  }
  size_t bytes_written = 0;
  i2s_write(kI2sPort, tx_i2s_words, word_index * sizeof(int32_t), &bytes_written, portMAX_DELAY);
}

/** Once the server has said the response is fully done AND the ring buffer has
    finished draining to the speaker, the reply has genuinely been heard in
    full - that's this step's actual PASS criterion, not just "bytes arrived". */
void checkPlaybackComplete() {
  if (!response_fully_received || !awaiting_reply) {
    return;
  }
  if (ring_used_bytes > 0) {
    return;  // still playing
  }
  markReplyReceived();
}

/** One-time setup: Wi-Fi, time sync, full-duplex mic+speaker I2S, LED, then
    open the secure WebSocket connection. */
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("[openai_playback] Boot.");
  Serial.printf("[openai_playback] Free heap at boot (baseline): %u, largest free block: %u\n",
                ESP.getFreeHeap(), ESP.getMaxAllocHeap());

  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  setLed(false);
  setupI2sFullDuplex();

  connectToWifi();
  syncSystemTime();

  Serial.println("[openai_playback] Opening WSS connection to the OpenAI Realtime API endpoint...");
  webSocket.onEvent(onWebSocketEvent);
  beginOpenAiRealtimeConnection(webSocket);
}

/** Services the WebSocket connection, sends session.update once flagged ready,
    watches for a LISTEN command, drains playback audio to the speaker, and
    checks for a reply timeout / playback completion. Unlike steps 9-11, this
    keeps running across many LISTEN cycles rather than halting after one
    verdict - it only ever stops if the connection itself can never be
    established. */
void loop() {
  if (!should_keep_running) {
    return;
  }

  webSocket.loop();

  if (should_send_session_update) {
    should_send_session_update = false;
    Serial.println("[openai_playback] Sending session.update...");
    webSocket.sendTXT(kSessionUpdateMessage);
  }

  checkForListenCommand();
  drainRingBufferToSpeaker();
  checkPlaybackComplete();
  checkReplyTimeout();
}
